1. Resources & Data Sources
    - Terraform configurations typically consist of resources (things Terraform creates and manages) and data sources (information Terraform reads from existing infrastructure).

2. Resource Block
    - A resource represents infrastructure that Terraform will create, update, and destroy.
    - Example: Create an S3 Bucket
        resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
            bucket = "my-unique-bucket-name-2024"
            tags = {
                Environment = "dev"
            }
        }
    - Resource Block Syntax
        resource "<PROVIDER_RESOURCE_TYPE>" "<LOCAL_NAME>" {
            # arguments
        }
    - Example:
        resource "aws_s3_bucket" "my_bucket" {
            bucket = "my-unique-bucket-name-2024"
        }
        aws_s3_bucket → Resource type.
        my_bucket → Local resource name used for references.
        Arguments inside the block configure the resource.

3. Resource References
    - Resources can reference attributes from other resources.
    - Example: Enable Versioning on the Bucket
        resource "aws_s3_bucket_versioning" "versioning" {
            bucket = aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.id
            versioning_configuration {
                status = "Enabled"
            }
        }
    - Reference Format
        <RESOURCE_TYPE>.<RESOURCE_NAME>.<ATTRIBUTE>
    - Example:
        aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.id
    - Terraform automatically detects this dependency and creates the bucket before enabling versioning.

4. Data Source Block
    - A data source reads information from existing infrastructure without managing it.
    - Terraform can use this information when creating new resources.
    - Example: Find the Latest Ubuntu 22.04 AMI
        data "aws_ami" "ubuntu" {
            most_recent = true
            owners      = ["099720109477"]
            filter {
                name   = "name"
                values = ["ubuntu/images/hvm-ssd/ubuntu-*-22.04-amd64-server-*"]
            }
        }
    - Data Source Syntax
        data "<DATA_TYPE>" "<LOCAL_NAME>" {
            # query arguments
        }
    - Unlike resources, data sources only read information.

5. Using Data Sources
    - The data source attributes can be referenced in resources.
        resource "aws_instance" "web" {
            ami           = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id
            instance_type = "t3.micro"
        }
    - Data Source Reference Format
        data.<DATA_TYPE>.<LOCAL_NAME>.<ATTRIBUTE>
    - Example:
        data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id
    - Terraform first queries AWS for the latest Ubuntu AMI and then uses the returned AMI ID when creating the EC2 instance.

6. Resource vs Data Source
    Feature	                        Resource	Data Source
    Creates infrastructure	        ✅ Yes	    ❌ No
    Updates infrastructure	        ✅ Yes	    ❌ No
    Deletes infrastructure	        ✅ Yes	    ❌ No
    Reads existing infrastructure	✅ Sometimes	✅ Yes
    Stored in Terraform state	    ✅ Yes	    Limited metadata only
    Managed by Terraform	        ✅ Yes	    ❌ No

7. Dependency Example
    data "aws_ami" "ubuntu" {
        most_recent = true
        owners      = ["099720109477"]
    }

    resource "aws_instance" "web" {
        ami           = data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id
        instance_type = "t3.micro"
    }
    - Execution order:
        Terraform reads the AMI using the data source.
        Terraform obtains the AMI ID.
        Terraform creates the EC2 instance using that AMI.

8. Key Takeaways
    Resources create and manage infrastructure.
    Data sources read existing infrastructure.
    Resource references use:
        aws_s3_bucket.my_bucket.id
    Data source references use:
        data.aws_ami.ubuntu.id
    Terraform automatically builds a dependency graph based on these references and executes operations in the correct order.